Polyfills in JavaScript are used to provide modern functionality on older browsers that do not support it. They are essentially JavaScript code that mimics the behavior of newer features so that they can be used in older environments.

For example, if a browser does not support the Array.from() method, a polyfill can be used to provide that functionality.

To use a polyfill, you can either include it manually in your code or use a tool like Babel or a library like core-js to automatically include the necessary polyfills.

Here's an example of a simple polyfill for Array.from():

if (!Array.from) {
  Array.from = function (arrayLike) {
    return Array.prototype.slice.call(arrayLike);
  };
}

This code checks if the Array.from() method exists. If it doesn't, it defines it using the slice() method of the Array prototype.

